<chapter xml:id="ch05-differentiation-rules-and-elementary-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Differentiation Rules and Elementary Functions</title>

  <introduction>
  <p>The derivative came from a limit:</p>

  <md>f'(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.</md>

  <p>That definition gives the meaning.  It gives instantaneous rate of change, tangent slope, and local linear approximation.  But it is too slow to use from the beginning every time.</p>

  <p>The last chapter already gave the first formulas:</p>

  <md>\frac{d}{dx}(c)=0,
\qquad
\frac{d}{dx}(x^n)=nx^{n-1}</md>

  <p>for positive integer powers <m>n</m>.  Now we turn those first formulas into a working language.</p>

  <p>A complicated function is often built from simpler functions.  The question is:</p>

  <md>\text{If we know how the pieces change, how does the whole expression change?}</md>
  </introduction>

